part.text:
Fixed. The bug was in `DiffException.prototype.recover` (src/diff/index.js:28): when a component render throws and the error is handled by an error boundary, the exception recovery only restored committed vnode pointers (`_dom`/`_children`) in the two narrow cases where they happened to already be equal (both branches were no-ops). The fallback path that restores `newVNode._dom = oldVNode._dom` and `newVNode._children = oldVNode._children` was missing, so when an error boundary re-renders a list item into a different vnode type (e.g. `TodoItemInner` → error `<li>`), the diff anchored against stale DOM pointers and left the old errored DOM in place alongside the new one.

The fix restores committed pointers unconditionally in the non-hydrating case (matching upstream preact behavior):

```js
} else {
    current.dom = previous.dom;
    current.children = previous.children;
}
```

Verified with a temporary reproduction test (added, ran, then removed) built from the issue scenario: it failed before the change (duplicated list item) and passes after. Full validation:
- all 124 `hooks/test/browser` tests pass
- 213 core render/component/error-boundary tests pass
- `test/shared` + `test/node` (mocha) pass
- the only failure in the full karma run (`events.test.js` focus-capture) is pre-existing and fails without my change too
